    id: "4e5bd01b3071b44acabf",
    cat: "geopolitica",
    title: "Letters with 'suspicious substances' sent to US senators and election offices",
    summary: "A number of states have confirmed they were targeted by the mailings, including Oklahoma, Ohio and Louisiana.",
    preview: "Federal authorities say they are investigating \"potentially hazardous\" letters containing \"suspicious substances\" that have been sent to congressional and state election offices around the US. The FBI and the US Postal Inspection Service (USPIS) said they were trying to trace the \"suspicious mailings\", some of which were intercepted before they were received. Authorities declined to say how many offices had been targeted, but several secretaries of state and congressional offices reported receiving the letters.",
    body: "Federal authorities say they are investigating \"potentially hazardous\" letters containing \"suspicious substances\" that have been sent to congressional and state election offices around the US.\n\nThe FBI and the US Postal Inspection Service (USPIS) said they were trying to trace the \"suspicious mailings\", some of which were intercepted before they were received.\n\nAuthorities declined to say how many offices had been targeted, but several secretaries of state and congressional offices reported receiving the letters.\n\nComing just weeks before the US midterm elections, it echoes previous security scares involving suspicious mail.\n\nThe suspicious letters were sent to at least five Democratic senators' offices, most of which were intercepted before delivery.\n\nThey include California's Alex Padilla, Connecticut's Chris Murphy and New York's Kirsten Gillibrand, the senators' offices confirmed to the BBC.\n\nNew York Senator Chuck Schumer and Delaware Senator Lisa Blunt Rochester were also sent the suspicious pieces of mail, according to the BBC's US partner CBS News.\n\n\"This incident is a stark reminder of the very real danger facing those who serve the public and who help administer our elections in the face of increasingly hostile political rhetoric,\" Padilla said in a statement to the BBC.\n\nSeveral state offices have also received the suspicious mailings.\n\nDelaware's Department of Elections said its office received and opened a letter containing a white powdery substance on Thursday, adding that staff followed security protocols on handling suspicious mail.\n\nThe Oklahoma State Election Board and the Louisiana secretary of state both said this week that their offices had received letters containing a white powder.\n\nThe Louisiana office was evacuated after a staff member opened the package on Tuesday, but it has since reopened after law enforcement found no evidence of harmful substances.\n\nOhio Secretary of State Frank LaRose said his office was the intended recipient of a letter containing white powder, but the USPIS had intercepted it.\n\nThe New York State Capitol in Albany and the Wyoming State Capitol in Cheyenne also received suspicious packages, according to emergency responder scanner feed reviewed by the BBC.\n\n\"This FBI and the US Postal Inspection Service will continue to safeguard the public and secure elections for American citizens,\" the agencies said in a statement on Thursday.\n\nThe suspicious mailings come just weeks before election day, which will determine the balance of power in Congress.\n\nAhead of the 2024 election, vote officials in 17 states received suspicious packages.\n\nA year earlier, suspicious envelopes, some laced with fentanyl, were sent to election offices in at least five states.\n\nRelated topics\n\n- Published3 days ago\n\n- Published6 October",
